=== Fully annotated: checked ===
  add(2, 3)       = 5
  add('a', 3)     -> TypeError: add(x=...): expected int, got str

=== Fully dynamic: Any silences everything ===
  sloppy('a', 'b') = 'ab'   (no complaint)
  sloppy('a', 3)  -> TypeError from PYTHON, not from the checker:
                     can only concatenate str (not "int") to str

=== Half typed: the boundary ===
  half_typed(1, 2) = 3
  half_typed('a','b') -> TypeError: half_typed(x=...): expected int, got str
  x was checked and rejected; y would have been let through.

Unsoundness, stated plainly: sloppy() passes the checker and still
blows up at runtime. That is gradual typing working as designed.
